<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ locale_tag() }}"
      data-theme="{{ current_user.theme|default('system') }}"
      data-density="{{ current_user.density|default('comfortable') }}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    {#
        One page name, in one place. `heading` is what the top bar puts in the
        <h1> and what this derives the tab's title from, so the two cannot drift
        apart and a page cannot end up titled one thing and headed another. A
        page with something else to say in the tab — a subscription's own name,
        say — overrides `title` as before.
    #}
    <title>{% block title %}{{ block('heading') }} — {{ instance_name }}{% endblock %}</title>
    {#
        One stylesheet, and two kinds of URL for the scripts beside it.

        `bundle()` looks a name up in the asset build's manifest and prints the
        content-hashed file the build produced — the design tokens, Tailwind's
        theme and utilities, every rule that dresses the application, and the
        @font-face rules for the vendored Inter. Until Phase 8 there was a
        second `<link>` here for a hand-written stylesheet served as written;
        that file moved into the build with the re-skin, because a token can
        only be the single definition of "brand" or "card" if the utility and
        the rule come out of the same compilation.

        `asset()` stamps a modification time onto the files that are still
        served exactly as written: the vendored htmx and the hand-written
        scripts. Both helpers answer the same question — what URL will a
        returning browser treat as new after an upgrade — for files that are
        produced two different ways.

        Every one of these URLs is served by this instance. Nothing on this
        page comes from a third-party host, and `assets:offline-check` fails
        the build if that stops being true.
    #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ bundle('app.css') }}">
    <script src="{{ asset('/assets/htmx.min.js') }}" defer></script>
    <script src="{{ asset('/assets/app.js') }}" defer></script>
    {# A module, so it is deferred already; `defer` on one is ignored. #}
    <script src="{{ bundle('app.js') }}" type="module"></script>
</head>
{#
    The CSRF token rides on every htmx request via hx-headers, so a fragment
    swap does not need a hidden field of its own.

    `has-shell` is what tells the stylesheet to leave room for the rail and the
    bottom bar. It is on the body rather than inferred from the presence of a
    sidebar, so the offset and the thing being offset around are decided by one
    condition — the same one that draws them.
#}
<body class="{{ current_user ? 'has-shell' : 'no-shell' }}"
      hx-headers='{"X-CSRF-Token": "{{ csrf_token() }}"}'>
{#
    The strings this page's JavaScript needs, from the same catalogue the
    server reads — see AppExtension::jsTranslations(). Rendered as JSON in a
    non-executing script tag rather than assigned in code, so a message
    containing a quote or a bracket cannot end up being parsed as JavaScript.
#}
<script id="renovo-messages" type="application/json">{{ js_translations()|raw }}</script>
<script>
    window.renovoI18n = (function () {
        var messages = {};

        try {
            messages = JSON.parse(document.getElementById('renovo-messages').textContent);
        } catch (error) {
            messages = {};
        }

        return {
            /* Named arguments only: the browser-side strings take no plurals. */
            t: function (key, params) {
                var message = messages[key] || key;

                if (params) {
                    Object.keys(params).forEach(function (name) {
                        message = message.split('{' + name + '}').join(params[name]);
                    });
                }

                return message;
            }
        };
    }());
</script>

<a class="skip-link" href="#main">{{ t('nav.skip_to_content') }}</a>

{#
    The shell, for a signed-in page: the rail, the top bar over the content and
    the bottom bar a narrow screen gets instead of the rail. Which item each of
    them marks as current is decided here, once, from the request path — so the
    highlight is right in the HTML that arrives rather than being corrected by
    script after the page has painted.

    Sign-in, registration and the setup wizard render without any of it. There
    is nowhere to navigate to before there is an account.
#}
{% if current_user %}
    {% set nav = navigation(scope, current_path) %}

    {% include 'partials/brand_sprite.twig' %}
    {% include 'partials/sidebar.twig' %}

    <header class="topbar">
        <div class="topbar-lead">
            {# The mark, for the widths where the rail that usually carries it is gone. #}
            <a class="topbar-brand" href="/" aria-label="{{ instance_name }}">
                <svg class="brand-mark" viewBox="0 0 460 427" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <use href="#renovo-mark"></use>
                </svg>
            </a>

            <h1 class="topbar-title">{% block heading %}{{ instance_name }}{% endblock %}</h1>

            {#
                What this particular page offers: editing the thing being
                viewed, going back to a list. The one action every page offers —
                adding a subscription — is in the actions beside it and is not
                repeated here.
            #}
            <div class="topbar-page-actions">{% block page_actions %}{% endblock %}</div>
        </div>

        {% include 'partials/topbar_actions.twig' %}
    </header>
{% endif %}

<main class="container" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    {% include 'partials/flashes.twig' %}
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
</main>

<footer class="container footer">
    <span>{{ instance_name }}</span>
    {% if current_user %}
        <button type="button" class="button-link" data-opens-dialog="shortcut-help">
            {{ t('shortcuts.title') }}
        </button>
    {% endif %}
</footer>

{% if current_user %}
    {% include 'partials/mobile_nav.twig' %}
    {% include 'partials/dialogs.twig' %}
{% endif %}
</body>
</html>
